# .aiqa/context.md — Repo-Level QA Context

> **Instructions:** Create this file at `.aiqa/context.md` in the ROOT of your
> test repository. It provides repo-level context that applies to ALL generated
> tests, regardless of product.

## Test repository overview

{Brief description of what this repo contains and how it is organised.}

Example: This is the E2E test suite for the Eroad Fleet Management platform.
Tests are grouped by product area under `tests/api`, `tests/ui`, and `tests/mobile`.

## Environment configuration

| Environment | Base URL | Notes |
|-------------|----------|-------|
| Local dev   | `http://localhost:8080` | Uses Docker Compose |
| CI/QA       | `https://qa.example.com` | Auto-deployed on PR |
| Staging     | `https://staging.example.com` | Manual deploy |

## Framework and conventions

- **API tests**: RestAssured + JUnit 5. Base class: `ApiTestBase`
- **UI tests**: Selenium 4 + JUnit 5. Base class: `UiTestBase`
- **Mobile tests**: Appium + JUnit 5. Base class: `MobileTestBase`
- **Assertions**: AssertJ only (no Hamcrest)
- **Test naming**: `should{ActionOrState}{WhenCondition}()`

## CI pipeline notes

- All tests tagged `@smoke` run on every PR
- Full regression runs nightly
- `@slow` tests are excluded from PR checks
- Flaky test retries: 2 attempts

## Authentication

- API tests use `TEST_API_TOKEN` env var (pre-seeded in CI)
- UI tests use `TEST_USERNAME` / `TEST_PASSWORD` env vars
- Never hardcode credentials in test files

## Important conventions

- Each test must be idempotent (safe to run multiple times)
- Tests must clean up their own test data (use `@AfterEach`)
- Use `@DisplayName("Given ... When ... Then ...")` for readable test output
- Avoid `Thread.sleep()` — use Awaitility for async assertions

